    id: "ab79741e882d4bd52653",
    cat: "conflitti",
    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "Jordan’s King Abdullah promises to confront ‘terrorists’ after Karak attack",
    summary: "King tells parliament attackers ‘are not of us’ and warns that Israeli expansionism is greatest threat to the region.",
    preview: "King tells parliament attackers ‘are not of us’ and warns that Israeli expansionism is greatest threat to the region. Jordan’s King Abdullah II says the kingdom would not be shaken by a deadly attack on a church in the southern city of Karak last month. Opening a new session of parliament in the capital, Amman, on Monday, Abdullah said “the treacherous hand of terrorism” had reached out “to test the resilience of our state and the resolve of our people”. “They are not of us,” he said of the attackers.",
    body: "King tells parliament attackers ‘are not of us’ and warns that Israeli expansionism is greatest threat to the region.\n\nJordan’s King Abdullah II says the kingdom would not be shaken by a deadly attack on a church in the southern city of Karak last month.\n\nOpening a new session of parliament in the capital, Amman, on Monday, Abdullah said “the treacherous hand of terrorism” had reached out “to test the resilience of our state and the resolve of our people”.\n\n“They are not of us,” he said of the attackers. “There is no place for them among us, not today and not tomorrow.”\n\nThe attack about two weeks ago killed nine people and wounded dozens. The king asked lawmakers to stand for a minute of silence for the victims, whose relatives attended the session.\n\nAl Jazeera’s Ahmed Jarrar, reporting from Amman, said Abdullah made clear that Jordan would confront “terrorism and extremism” by relying first on national unity and public awareness, backed by the army and security services, which would strike “with an iron fist”.\n\nAbdullah also addressed regional tensions, warning that Israeli expansionism is the greatest threat to the Middle East.\n\n“Israeli expansionist ambitions are the greatest threat to the future of the region and its security,” he said, adding: “The humanitarian catastrophe in Gaza continues, and successive assaults persist in the West Bank and Jerusalem.”\n\nThe king also pledged to protect Jordan’s scarce water supplies, Jarrar said.\n\n“We will not allow the thirst of Jordanians to be held hostage by any party,” he said. Jarrar said this reflected concerns that Israeli projects threaten the kingdom’s water security.\n\nJordan, a close United States ally, signed a peace treaty with Israel in 1994, and millions of its citizens are of Palestinian origin.\n\nJarrar said the king’s third message was directed at lawmakers as he stressed that the nation is the source of authority and debate in parliament is healthy as long as it serves the national interest.\n\n“Your differences of opinion are a strength so long as Jordan’s interest is the goal,” Abdullah said, urging the government to improve living conditions.\n\nHe also warned officials against abusing their positions. “The law knows no name, no position and no influence,” he said.",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "Al Jazeera",
    url: "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/2/jordans-king-abdullah-promises-to-confront-terrorists-after-karak-attack?traffic_source=rss",
